  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday morning through next Thursday)\nIssued at 220 PM EDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nThe heaviest of the rain will start off the long term period as a\nstationary front associated with a weak trough makes its way down\nacross the south that should be primed for rainfall. PW values \ncould exceed two inches overnight Sunday and into Monday morning. \nThe models have come into better agreement on where the heaviest \nprecip is most likely to occur, but there is still some \nuncertainty as it largely depends on how far south the front makes\nit. At this time, it looks like the heaviest axis of precip will \nbe along and south of I-85. While we're expecting widespread 1-3\" \nin this area, a few spots could see more than 4\" which could cause\nlocalized flash flooding as well as river flooding as the rain \ndrags into Monday. \n\nThe front lingers across southern Georgia through the start of \nnext work week. There is some uncertainty as to how much rain the \nvery southern counties of our CWA will get as we move into next \nweek. Forecast totals range from a half an inch to an additional \ntwo inches until the front is pushed off to the east on Wednesday.\n\nFor a majority of the area however, rain is expected to come to \nan end overnight Monday and into Tuesday morning. Dry air will \nsettle in across much of the area north of a line from Columbus to\nAugusta with high temperatures in the mid to upper 70s. Overnight\nlows will fall into the 50s with 40s likely in the higher \nelevations of northern Georgia.",
